02:27:23,180 --> 02:27:24,280
We're sending data?

2775
02:27:25,060 --> 02:27:25,580
From...

2776
02:27:25,580 --> 02:27:26,420
Oh, my God.

2777
02:27:26,920 --> 02:27:29,100
And the cool thing is, it's being sent...

2778
02:27:29,100 --> 02:27:31,860
via the relay up there, and then back to Kerben.

2779
02:27:32,440 --> 02:27:33,700
Actually, we have two hops.

2780
02:27:34,680 --> 02:27:35,700
They're going both...

2781
02:27:35,700 --> 02:27:37,080
They're going through both relays right now.

2782
02:27:38,020 --> 02:27:39,900
Dude, we're descending so slowly.

2783
02:27:41,740 --> 02:27:43,140
They look like soap bubbles.

2784
02:27:44,500 --> 02:27:45,600
I'm going to fast forward.

2785
02:27:46,640 --> 02:27:47,720
Fast forward, dude!

2786
02:27:48,160 --> 02:27:49,660
Let me get down to the surface!

2787
02:27:51,080 --> 02:27:52,360
Let me measure the temperature.

2788
02:27:56,040 --> 02:27:57,000
Eve's shallows.

2789
02:27:58,060 --> 02:28:01,400
I tell you what is shallow, and that's the atmosphere temperature gradient.

2790
02:28:02,080 --> 02:28:02,720
Oh, yeah?

2791
02:28:03,320 --> 02:28:04,860
Is this like a greenhouse effect?

2792
02:28:04,860 --> 02:28:05,620
Is this like Venus?

2793
02:28:06,940 --> 02:28:07,620
Transmit.

2794
02:28:08,760 --> 02:28:10,500
We have plenty of electric charge.

2795
02:28:13,120 --> 02:28:14,640
Seismic accelerometer.

2796
02:28:17,580 --> 02:28:17,960
Display?

2797
02:28:19,900 --> 02:28:20,660
Oh!

2798
02:28:20,900 --> 02:28:22,160
It's measuring the G-forces.

2799
02:28:26,270 --> 02:28:27,690
Seismic scan can't be done right now.

2800
02:28:27,790 --> 02:28:29,210
I guess we've got to be on the ground, then.

2801
02:28:29,750 --> 02:28:30,710
That would make sense to me.

2802
02:28:30,870 --> 02:28:31,230
Oh, God.

2803
02:28:31,290 --> 02:28:32,510
Look at this big boy right here.

2804
02:28:34,810 --> 02:28:35,830
Uh, mystery goo.

2805
02:28:36,030 --> 02:28:37,370
Actually, maybe I do that on the surface.

2806
02:28:37,570 --> 02:28:38,530
It's probably worth more.

2807
02:28:39,550 --> 02:28:41,410
Are we going to have a safe landing spot here, even?

2808
02:28:42,310 --> 02:28:43,190
What even is that?

2809
02:28:44,630 --> 02:28:45,270
There's life!

2810
02:28:47,310 --> 02:28:48,390
There's plants, for sure!

2811
02:28:50,990 --> 02:28:52,730
Maybe they have, uh...

2812
02:28:52,730 --> 02:28:54,070
red kerbals down here.

2813
02:28:57,330 --> 02:28:58,710
Pan-spermia is real.

2814
02:28:59,210 --> 02:28:59,790
Oh, my God.

2815
02:29:02,560 --> 02:29:03,100
Stone!

2816
02:29:03,600 --> 02:29:05,400
On a different planet, baby!

2817
02:29:06,280 --> 02:29:07,780
Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha!

2818
02:29:08,580 --> 02:29:10,140
I can't believe it, man.

2819
02:29:10,240 --> 02:29:10,780
We did it.

2820
02:29:11,340 --> 02:29:12,200
Hell, yeah.

2821
02:29:13,760 --> 02:29:14,840
Oh, man.

2822
02:29:15,000 --> 02:29:15,640
Oh, man.

2823
02:29:16,220 --> 02:29:17,700
I can barely see shit.

2824
02:29:17,980 --> 02:29:19,740
All these plants are in the way in my shoots.

2825
02:29:20,020 --> 02:29:20,180
Okay.

2826
02:29:20,940 --> 02:29:22,520
Can I actually get rid of them?

2827
02:29:25,960 --> 02:29:26,820
Deploy mode.

2828
02:29:28,540 --> 02:29:29,900
I'm going to lock pressure data.

2829
02:29:31,220 --> 02:29:32,480
From Eve's shallows.

2830
02:29:32,980 --> 02:29:36,820
The instrument shows you an extremely high pressure and then refuses to work anymore.

2831
02:29:39,760 --> 02:29:40,300
Send it.

2832
02:29:41,560 --> 02:29:43,840
Dude, we're going to get so much science out of this, too!

2833
02:29:44,960 --> 02:29:45,900
Oh, it's dope!

2834
02:29:46,080 --> 02:29:47,260
Man, I love sending science.

2835
02:29:47,500 --> 02:29:48,660
This may be my favorite pastime.

2836
02:29:49,600 --> 02:29:50,400
Okay, time for the goo.

2837
02:29:53,000 --> 02:29:54,260
Let's see what we got here.

2838
02:29:55,860 --> 02:29:59,600
The goo has produced a cooler and offers you a tiny cold beer.

2839
02:29:59,840 --> 02:30:00,000
What?

2840
02:30:00,940 --> 02:30:01,340
What?

2841
02:30:01,760 --> 02:30:04,060
I mean, I'll crack over the cold one here.

2842
02:30:04,820 --> 02:30:06,040
That sounds awesome.

2843
02:30:06,400 --> 02:30:07,080
Transmit that.

2844
02:30:07,740 --> 02:30:07,800
Oh!

2845
02:30:08,620 --> 02:30:10,900
Transmitting will render the module inoperable.

2846
02:30:10,900 --> 02:30:11,400
Yeah, that's fine.

2847
02:30:12,840 --> 02:30:13,820
That's by design.

2848
02:30:14,240 --> 02:30:14,660
Okay.

2849
02:30:15,020 --> 02:30:15,660
It's all good.

2850
02:30:16,880 --> 02:30:20,700
The question is, though, are there going to be hostile aliens anywhere nearby?

2851
02:30:20,720 --> 02:30:21,780
That's what I'm worried about.

2852
02:30:22,920 --> 02:30:23,920
Wait, can I do this again?

2853
02:30:25,360 --> 02:30:27,060
Sun, sea, and sand are all here.

2854
02:30:27,240 --> 02:30:28,920
And the temperature is lovely and warm to boot.

2855
02:30:30,640 --> 02:30:31,060
Uploaded.

2856
02:30:32,540 --> 02:30:33,620
13 signs at it!

2857
02:30:34,100 --> 02:30:34,580
Can I do it again?

2858
02:30:36,860 --> 02:30:39,860
No, but I can do this, but I'm not getting home with this thing.

2859
02:30:40,860 --> 02:30:41,920
What about this guy now?

2860
02:30:43,960 --> 02:30:46,140
Display is 0 point...

2861
02:30:46,140 --> 02:30:47,440
1.7G, I guess.

2862
02:30:49,620 --> 02:30:50,360
That makes sense.

2863
02:30:52,420 --> 02:30:56,300
I guess so, if the planet is bigger than Kerbin, which I maybe think it is?

2864
02:30:56,400 --> 02:30:56,700
I'm not sure.

2865
02:30:58,400 --> 02:30:58,980
Seismic data?

2866
02:30:59,440 --> 02:30:59,820
Oh!

2867
02:31:01,060 --> 02:31:05,680
The vibrations, though slight, might be enough to map the interior structure of the planet.

2868
02:31:07,980 --> 02:31:08,640
Oh, yeah?

2869
02:31:09,660 --> 02:31:11,520
Is it going to have a molten core?

2870
02:31:12,580 --> 02:31:13,060
Transmit.

2871
02:31:14,260 --> 02:31:15,180
Oh, that's a lot.

2872
02:31:15,260 --> 02:31:15,380
Look.

2873
02:31:17,360 --> 02:31:19,440
Yeah, we're running out of electric charge on that one.

2874
02:31:20,080 --> 02:31:21,440
Not enough electric charge.

2875
02:31:23,940 --> 02:31:26,720
So it's just going to keep sending while it charges here.

2876
02:31:27,400 --> 02:31:28,820
Let me fast forward through that.

2877
02:31:32,470 --> 02:31:32,790
60%.

2878
02:31:36,780 --> 02:31:37,540
Oh, wait a minute.

2879
02:31:37,960 --> 02:31:39,340
Oh, I thought we lost signal for a second.

2880
02:31:40,900 --> 02:31:41,220
92%.

2881
02:31:42,820 --> 02:31:43,140
100%.

2882
02:31:43,520 --> 02:31:43,840
Nice.

2883
02:31:44,960 --> 02:31:45,780
What else can I do?

2884
02:31:48,540 --> 02:31:50,260
Obviously, no crew report when there was no crew.

2885
02:31:51,180 --> 02:31:52,060
Kerb net access?

2886
02:31:52,400 --> 02:31:53,400
I mean, we're on the surface now.

2887
02:31:53,520 --> 02:31:55,500
That's not going to do much, is it?

2888
02:31:57,880 --> 02:32:01,240
Man, this almost looks like an underwater world, doesn't it?

2889
02:32:03,100 --> 02:32:04,300
I can hear wind, too.

2890
02:32:05,780 --> 02:32:06,120
Oh, look!

2891
02:32:06,260 --> 02:32:06,580
A little...

2892
02:32:06,580 --> 02:32:06,740
No!

2893
02:32:06,840 --> 02:32:08,280
We were so close to the water!

2894
02:32:08,860 --> 02:32:09,200
Wait.

2895
02:32:09,880 --> 02:32:10,220
Guys.

2896
02:32:15,810 --> 02:32:16,670
Oh, no!

2897
02:32:17,190 --> 02:32:18,490
We don't have a big enough...

2898
02:32:18,490 --> 02:32:19,450
What do you call it?

2899
02:32:21,290 --> 02:32:21,990
The wheel.

2900
02:32:22,390 --> 02:32:23,070
Reaction wheel.

2901
02:32:23,210 --> 02:32:25,610
I would have loved to try and roll down the hill.

2902
02:32:25,790 --> 02:32:26,750
Because we're a disc.

2903
02:32:29,050 --> 02:32:29,970
That would be cool.

2904
02:32:31,230 --> 02:32:31,670
Man.

2905
02:32:32,550 --> 02:32:32,990
Man.

2906
02:32:34,070 --> 02:32:35,150
Let me see what else I can do.

2907
02:32:35,470 --> 02:32:36,430
Did I get all the science?

2908
02:32:37,290 --> 02:32:37,970
Pressure data.

2909
02:32:38,710 --> 02:32:39,690
That one we did.

2910
02:32:40,530 --> 02:32:42,510
Now, we can't do the goo again, unfortunately.

2911
02:32:42,830 --> 02:32:44,470
And that one we did already.

2912
02:32:46,410 --> 02:32:47,450
Dude, amazing.

2913
02:32:47,970 --> 02:32:48,410
Unbelievable.

2914
02:32:49,850 --> 02:32:50,330
Unbelievable.

2915
02:32:51,550 --> 02:32:55,050
We have officially made landfall on another planet.

2916
02:32:56,550 --> 02:32:57,390
And you know what?

2917
02:32:57,390 --> 02:32:58,010
We're not done.

2918
02:32:58,070 --> 02:32:58,910
Because now we're going home.

2919
02:33:00,290 --> 02:33:03,470
The V3 relay is about to come home.

2920
02:33:06,270 --> 02:33:08,150
But not before getting some science from orbit.

2921
02:33:10,610 --> 02:33:11,250
Here we go.

2922
02:33:12,070 --> 02:33:13,350
Do all the science we can.

2923
02:33:15,110 --> 02:33:16,370
We're going to take temperature measurements.

2924
02:33:17,670 --> 02:33:19,350
Oh, that one's not going to do much, I guess.

2925
02:33:19,410 --> 02:33:19,670
Wait a minute.

2926
02:33:21,250 --> 02:33:22,450
I just realized something, guys.

2927
02:33:25,330 --> 02:33:26,750
How am I even going to get home with this?

2928
02:33:27,410 --> 02:33:28,430
There's no parachute on this.

2929
02:33:28,450 --> 02:33:29,170
I didn't think about that.

2930
02:33:31,030 --> 02:33:31,370
Fuck.

2931
02:33:33,090 --> 02:33:33,990
Okay, check it out.

2932
02:33:34,110 --> 02:33:34,750
You know what we're going to do?

2933
02:33:36,210 --> 02:33:37,530
We're going to land on Minmus.

2934
02:33:38,270 --> 02:33:38,870
That's right.

2935
02:33:39,170 --> 02:33:40,650
We're going to land on Minmus.

2936
02:33:41,450 --> 02:33:42,290
But wait, fuck.

2937
02:33:42,370 --> 02:33:43,210
We have no landing legs.

2938
02:33:45,090 --> 02:33:45,730
It's okay.

2939
02:33:46,030 --> 02:33:47,350
We're going to land on Minmus.

2940
02:33:47,510 --> 02:33:49,870
Very, very, very...

2941
02:33:50,430 --> 02:33:50,830
Very...

2942
02:33:50,830 --> 02:33:51,630
What's the word?

2943
02:33:53,190 --> 02:33:54,110
You know, gently.

2944
02:33:55,310 --> 02:33:58,750
And then we're going to send some Kerbals up there to Minmus to collect the science.

2945
02:34:00,330 --> 02:34:01,410
Oh, my God.

2946
02:34:02,830 --> 02:34:04,210
It's going to be good.

2947
02:34:04,950 --> 02:34:05,870
Lock the temperature.

2948
02:34:06,230 --> 02:34:06,690
Grab it.

2949
02:34:07,350 --> 02:34:08,050
What else we got?

2950
02:34:09,250 --> 02:34:10,270
What's back here?

2951
02:34:12,030 --> 02:34:12,630
Magnetometer.

2952
02:34:12,850 --> 02:34:13,510
I forgot about this.

2953
02:34:14,570 --> 02:34:15,270
Extend the boom.

2954
02:34:18,290 --> 02:34:19,130
That's dope.

2955
02:34:21,370 --> 02:34:21,970
Wow.

2956
02:34:21,970 --> 02:34:27,270
I guess this will tell us whether or not Eve is...

2957
02:34:28,430 --> 02:34:32,350
Or it has a molten core that is rotating.

2958
02:34:34,110 --> 02:34:34,850
Let's see.

2959
02:34:36,430 --> 02:34:42,390
You've measured the interaction between the solar wind and the magnetic field of this body as it deflects charged particles.

2960
02:34:43,270 --> 02:34:43,790
Confirmed!

2961
02:34:43,910 --> 02:34:46,350
It has a rotating molten core of metal.

2962
02:34:47,810 --> 02:34:48,330
Lovely.

2963
02:34:48,810 --> 02:34:50,090
That's a good sign, too.

2964
02:34:50,290 --> 02:34:57,030
Because that means the Kerbals can probably safely land on the planet without getting too many, you know, charged particles up in their system.

2965
02:34:57,490 --> 02:35:00,370
Now, of course, we have the five atmosphere pressure.

2966
02:35:00,850 --> 02:35:01,870
That's a different thing.

2967
02:35:02,590 --> 02:35:05,350
Now, do I transmit or keep it?

2968
02:35:06,450 --> 02:35:08,070
Keeping it is greedy.

2969
02:35:09,690 --> 02:35:12,650
Keeping it means landing on Minmus.

2970
02:35:14,510 --> 02:35:15,810
Transmitting is guaranteed.

2971
02:35:17,450 --> 02:35:18,090
Hmm.

2972
02:35:19,710 --> 02:35:21,170
I think I'll transmit.

2973
02:35:21,390 --> 02:35:26,490
And the reason is we can run the experiment again at different points of our journey.

2974
02:35:27,330 --> 02:35:28,390
I'm going to send.

2975
02:35:32,560 --> 02:35:34,340
Plenty of electric charge for that.

2976
02:35:34,340 --> 02:35:35,200
But look at that.

2977
02:35:37,760 --> 02:35:38,280
Nice.

2978
02:35:39,940 --> 02:35:41,380
Did I bring more science?

2979
02:35:42,260 --> 02:35:43,020
Pressure data.

2980
02:35:44,360 --> 02:35:45,900
Eve watches you malevolently.

2981
02:35:46,120 --> 02:35:47,100
Yeah, I watched back.

2982
02:35:48,860 --> 02:35:50,720
I think we already measured the temperature.

2983
02:35:51,500 --> 02:35:52,360
Yes, we did.

2984
02:35:53,520 --> 02:35:54,040
Okay.

2985
02:35:54,800 --> 02:35:56,240
Let's see if we can't get home.

2986
02:35:57,920 --> 02:35:59,320
How is this even going to work?

2987
02:36:03,120 --> 02:36:04,100
Let's see what we are.

2988
02:36:05,560 --> 02:36:07,740
Yo, Kerbin is actually...

2989
02:36:09,280 --> 02:36:10,360
...behind us.

2990
02:36:11,280 --> 02:36:12,720
Which is not good.

2991
02:36:13,560 --> 02:36:15,280
That's actually really bad.

2992
02:36:18,330 --> 02:36:18,950
Hmm.

2993
02:36:20,990 --> 02:36:23,430
Ideally, Kerbin would have to be...

2994
02:36:23,430 --> 02:36:25,490
...well, I guess, plus...

2995
02:36:25,490 --> 02:36:27,070
...54 degrees here.

2996
02:36:28,010 --> 02:36:28,630
Right?

2997
02:36:30,390 --> 02:36:32,430
Which is not going to happen for a long time.

2998
02:36:34,210 --> 02:36:35,090
So we could...

2999
02:36:35,090 --> 02:36:35,830
We know what we could do.

3000
02:36:35,910 --> 02:36:37,130
We could leave it here for a while.

3001
02:36:39,390 --> 02:36:41,290
Or we just brute force it.

3002
02:36:44,400 --> 02:36:45,580
3,000 delta V.

3003
02:36:46,820 --> 02:36:47,880
A little more than that.

3004
02:36:47,980 --> 02:36:49,360
3,500 delta V.

3005
02:36:51,120 --> 02:36:52,620
I don't know if we can actually do that.

3006
02:36:56,560 --> 02:36:57,660
Let me see my objectives.

3007
02:36:59,720 --> 02:37:00,200
Oh, wait.

3008
02:37:00,260 --> 02:37:01,340
We didn't even do this anyway.

3009
02:37:02,460 --> 02:37:03,760
This was part of this one.

3010
02:37:03,900 --> 02:37:04,980
Oh, no.

3011
02:37:04,980 --> 02:37:05,080
No.

3012
02:37:07,160 --> 02:37:07,580
Guys.

3013
02:37:11,060 --> 02:37:11,900
Oh, shit.

3014
02:37:13,360 --> 02:37:15,320
But I guess doing one of them will...

3015
02:37:15,320 --> 02:37:16,520
...stay permanent, right?

3016
02:37:16,760 --> 02:37:18,780
If I do this one and not the other...

3017
02:37:18,780 --> 02:37:20,120
...it's going to stay done.

3018
02:37:20,600 --> 02:37:22,060
So that my next trip...

3019
02:37:22,060 --> 02:37:23,220
...we're going to splash down.

3020
02:37:23,380 --> 02:37:24,480
And then it's going to be complete.

3021
02:37:26,560 --> 02:37:28,480
Okay, maybe we just leave it here, actually, then.

3022
02:37:30,520 --> 02:37:30,940
Yeah.

3023
02:37:34,130 --> 02:37:35,610
Eve has been conquered.

3024
02:37:36,970 --> 02:37:38,390
Kerbals on this day?

3025
02:37:39,670 --> 02:37:41,030
I can't see the date.

3026
02:37:42,850 --> 02:37:44,610
It's somewhere in year two.

3027
02:37:45,460 --> 02:37:47,130
They have made it to Minmus.

3028
02:37:47,350 --> 02:37:48,630
Albeit with probes.

3029
02:37:48,950 --> 02:37:50,630
And we landed, damn it.

3030
02:37:50,990 --> 02:37:52,790
We touched the atmosphere.

3031
02:37:53,270 --> 02:37:55,450
We felt the breeze...

3032
02:37:55,450 --> 02:37:57,210
...between the electronic systems.

3033
02:37:57,630 --> 02:37:59,970
And we saw the soap bubbles...

3034
02:37:59,970 --> 02:38:00,850
...on the surface.

3035
02:38:00,850 --> 02:38:03,450
And we did indeed transmit science back.

3036
02:38:03,790 --> 02:38:05,530
Now, we did not land in water...

3037
02:38:05,530 --> 02:38:06,930
...but we did land on land.

3038
02:38:07,210 --> 02:38:09,110
And somehow that's even more impressive.

3039
02:38:09,470 --> 02:38:11,410
Given the fact that land is not soft.

3040
02:38:11,590 --> 02:38:12,510
Land is hard.

3041
02:38:13,650 --> 02:38:15,210
And that's why we do these things.

3042
02:38:22,860 --> 02:38:23,380
Wonderful.

3043
02:38:23,800 --> 02:38:24,240
Good mission.

3044
02:38:24,800 --> 02:38:25,340
Thumbs up.

3045
02:38:29,290 --> 02:38:29,810
Okay.

3046
02:38:30,370 --> 02:38:31,490
What do I do next?

3047
02:38:31,890 --> 02:38:32,870
Let me see my science.

3048
02:38:33,610 --> 02:38:34,450
570 science.

3049
